\title{Disjointness bounds the rank at an interpolation node}
\taxon{Lemma}
\meta{status}{proof-account}
\meta{dependencies}{[Polynomial setup](cn-0019); [Evaluation identity](cn-0020)}
\meta{provenance}{[OpenAI, 23 September 2026](cn-0042), equation (2.5); reconstructed as a reading account on 9 October 2026. [Lean source: NodeRankLoss](/source-material/openai-math/lean/OAI/Combinatorics/Crossing/NodeRankLoss.lean).}

\p{\strong{Statement.} In the [polynomial setup](cn-0019), suppose #{U_j\cap V_j=0}. Then}

##{\dim E_h(W;t_j)\leq k_h(W)-k_h(V_j).}

\p{\strong{Proof.} Let #{\pi_j:W\to W/V_j} be the quotient map. For any non-node #{t}, its kernel on #{E_h(W;t)} contains #{E_h(V_j;t)}. [The evaluation identity](cn-0020) gives}

##{\dim\pi_j(E_h(W;t))\leq k_h(W)-k_h(V_j).}

\p{Choose a fixed basis of #{\mathcal P_h(W)}, evaluate it at the variable #{\xi}, and project by #{\pi_j}. This is a polynomial matrix. Every minor exceeding the displayed rank bound vanishes at all non-nodes, an infinite set, and is therefore the zero polynomial. The same rank bound holds at #{t_j}. Finally #{E_h(W;t_j)\subseteq U_j}, and #{\pi_j} is injective on #{U_j} by [disjointness](cn-0076). Thus projection does not reduce its dimension there.}

\p{The proof uses vanishing polynomial minors to specialize the rank bound. It does not assume that a generic basis stays independent at a constraint node.}
